/*
 * Lower.h.ct - TIR -> LIR for the S1b-S2 subset (contract §4).
 *
 * What it lowers, and the rule for everything else:
 *
 *   functions with a body, plain parameters (no `ref` / `out`), a return type
 *   that does not own a handle, and bodies built from declarations with one
 *   declarator, assignment / expression statements, `return`, `if`, `while`,
 *   `for`, `break`, `continue`, blocks, empty statements, `goto` / labels in
 *   one block, `defer` / `errdefer` and AutoDrop cleanups;
 *   expressions: literals, names, field / arrow, index, deref, address, cast,
 *   unary, binary, plain calls, assignment, postfix / prefix, ternary.
 *
 * Anything outside that set marks the *function* `ok = 0` and the module
 * `ready = 0`. The verifier still runs over the functions that did lower (so
 * V5-V8 are exercised on the real corpus), but the program is emitted by the
 * old AST path, byte for byte. That fallback is the whole safety argument of
 * this stage: `--ir=on` never prints a program it did not fully lower.
 *
 * Every exit gets its cleanup set materialised as statements:
 *
 *   exit(scope) = per scope inner -> outer, deferred entries in reverse
 *                 source order, then drop / release / handback entries in
 *                 reverse source order (§4.3). An exit only sees the entries
 *                 already registered at its source position; the block-tail
 *                 flush sees every entry of its own scope.
 *
 * A `return` with an active cleanup materialises its value into
 * `__ct_retN` first (SH-45 / D38-3), and the current emitter allocates that
 * number from one module-wide counter -- not per function as contract §4.2
 * says. Byte-identical output wins; the difference is recorded in the stage
 * notes. `break` / `continue` become `Jump` statements carrying their own
 * cleanup set.
 *
 * Printing is the other half of the unit: `ct_lir_emit` writes prototypes,
 * then definitions, in source order. It only implements the subset above,
 * but it must be byte-identical for it; `test/ir/lir-run.sh` compares the
 * two paths on the S2 fixtures.
 *
 * License: same as the rest of the compiler (see LICENSE / LICENSE.EXCEPTION).
 */

